We could do a BFS from each cell to find the distance to the closest target color. This would take O(R * C * (R + C)) time. We can do better.

This is a perfect use case for multisource BFS, since it allows us to find the closest cell of a specific color to every other cell in a single traversal.

Multisource BFS is like a BFS except that, at the beginning, we add multiple starting nodes to the queue and set their distances to 0. From there, the main BFS loop works as usual, this time expanding from all starting nodes at the same pace. The visit order will be from closest to furthest from any starting node.

In this case, the set of 'sources' are the cells of a given color. We can do this for each color.

We can use the multisource BFS recipe, but we'll need to adapt it to grid graphs:

def multisource_BFS(graph, sources):
  Q = Queue()
  distances = {}
  for start in sources:
    Q.push(start)
    distances[start] = 0
  while not Q.empty(): # Normal BFS loop
    node = Q.pop()
    for nbr in graph[node]:
      if nbr not in distances:
        distances[nbr] = distances[node] + 1
        Q.push(nbr)

